[Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  so  [Music]  [Music]  so  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  so  [Music]  [Music]  so  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  so  [Music]  so  [Music]  hey everyone i'm brian wood i'm the pr  manager  here at wolfram uh today i'm here with  danielle mayer  director of communications and outreach  how you doing today danielle  i'm fantastic how are you brian i am  excellent thank you for asking  uh danielle's here to talk a little bit  about remote work and remote learning  um this is a little bit of a different  topic from some of our usual tech talks  uh instead of necessarily talking just  about wolfram technology we're going to  talk about some of the  technology and the um strategies that we  use as a company  to kind of keep things moving in a  remote workforce  which obviously is a topic that is on  everyone's mind right now  since everything has gone virtual um  but uh i'd like to start with you know  we at wolf room have sort of been doing  remote work for  a little while like this isn't something  necessarily entirely new for us  so could you talk a little bit about  that like what kinds of things are we  already  have we already kind of been doing uh in  the past years  oh absolutely so we've always had some  flexibility  for certain positions so if  for instance developers that work on  code don't necessarily need to be in the  same room  in a collaborative environment so we've  we've allowed a lot of flexibility  for where people are located even if  people aren't located in the same place  all the time  we've we've let them work from home or  from whatever  you know place that works for them  depending on their their situation but  of course we've always maintained  an actual home office where those  collaborative meetings and  in the teams that need to come together  and see face to face  were were primarily stations um you know  march 12th came around and and everybody  went home  and now it's it was a struggle it was it  was now what do we do we don't  have these face-to-face meetings we  can't book a conference room and sit  around a table  and and work out whatever the the  current issue is at the time  so that that was the transition part  right right  yeah and and it's uh you know i can  speak to  being near the office it was always kind  of a  you know you can you can go in you can  work at home  most people would go into the office if  given the chance you know  um but now it's it's entirely remote uh  and that's that's definitely been a bit  of a transition for everyone i think  um but you know at the same time i think  there are  some advantages to working from remote  at least in some cases  uh although again this is sort of a bit  of a force situation but  could you speak to some of the some of  the things that you think are  good about people being able to work  from remote  absolutely so one of the most common  things that we've heard is  just the time saving you aren't  getting ready in the morning you aren't  doing a commute you don't have to drop  the kids off somewhere and then go pick  up something and then go into the office  your work time is you sit down at your  desk and that's your work time so  hopefully that um that would normally  free up a good amount of somebody's day  right uh where they aren't having to do  those things of course  we'll see how that plays out depending  on you know how how people are  getting their kids back to school or  daycare or all of the other things  that that we have to deal with right now  yeah yeah so there's all of that a lot  of those kinds of time-saving and  uh sort of energy-saving things in  theory um  even though it's it's a little bit up in  the air right now  um so what kinds of uh what kinds of  technology  do you find useful in terms of being  able to communicate being able to keep  up with everything  and uh sort of using your daily workflow  since since we've all gone remote  yeah so the the flexibility i think is  key because not the same thing works for  every single person  so i have teams that i meet with on zoom  where we do video because seeing  somebody's reaction  and seeing how passionate somebody is or  how upset somebody is  those are those are clues verbal clues  that are  really needed in some situations and  with some groups of people  um then of course there's there's the  everyday  decisions need to be made approvals need  to be given you have to look at this  thing real quick say yes or no  we use a lot of our instant messaging  platform for that  and that helps not have to track  somebody down  one of the things i was known for when  we were in the office is  if i needed something from you i was  going to show up at your door  i was going to be standing outside your  office like hello i need this thing  um and i can't do that anymore so now  it's well when do i pick up the phone  the phone feels so weird right now it's  just something that we haven't used very  much but now i'm starting to try to do a  voice thing if i can't get somebody on  zoom to go face to face  um or you know like i said the instant  message those are our primary methods of  communication right now right and of  course  you know the regular things um email and  uh  ticketing software things like that that  any any business would normally use  um but yeah it is it's almost kind of  surprising when you get used to  uh these zoom meetings and then um  suddenly you get into one where  everybody's actually  has their face showing and and you  realize that oh  yeah it's a lot easier to kind of read  people's reactions pick up on cues  um and i honestly i think it can make  meetings go faster sometimes because  there's  it's kind of easier to understand each  other a little bit better so  yeah i'm i'm definitely a fan of the uh  uh  getting face to face to the extent that  we can even though it's kind of tough  these days  um but of course that for for us it's  not just  um it's not just the everyday workflow  there's also  like events and and a lot of other  things that we've sort of  been having to rethink uh so you could  just talk talk  just a little bit about the events and  things  absolutely so um at the beginning you  know when we're like okay everybody's  doing this transition from home  we wanted to focus on making sure that  people had the tools that they needed  to make that transition so i know our  sales team had a big effort where they  were  basically saying if you need our cloud  or online technology  we'll give it to you for free here's  your transition  time to try to figure out how to make  this work from home  because not everybody had the background  that we had as a company to just  flip that switch so once  that immediate fire was was kind of  figured out we said what else are we  missing right now  and that that level of interaction and  engagement was something that we were  worried about losing  from our in-person events especially um  you know we have  our annual summer school and summer camp  which  we bring everybody to a college campus  and they stay in the dorms and  and you stay up late and you know drink  coffee and code and  together in an environment like that um  you know our wolfram u courses you know  a lot of them have always been online  but now that everybody's spending eight  hours or more a day  online did they really want to join  another  zoom session or get on the computer  again for another thing  so we came up with an idea of these  study groups  and what that was designed to do is  bring  people away from the online  technology you're being lectured to  you're staring at a screen getting stuff  delivered to you which everybody was now  quickly getting sick of doing sure so  the  the study groups the the concept behind  it was  bring people together let them turn on  their cameras let them ask  questions and really be engaged and work  on things together  and that was an environment that we  think really did take off we had a lot  of people that were really excited to be  able to have  that interaction that i think was  missing from their lives at that point  in time  yeah yeah i've heard a lot of good  things about the study groups and um  sort of the variety of people  that have showed up i think that's um to  me that  sort of speaks to a rich educational  environment like people  learning from people who are a little  bit different from them because you get  some of the uh you know older  professionals or  hobbyists or people who maybe have never  even touched on the subject  then you also have the the students and  the teachers and  you know it's uh i i i've heard that  lots of people have  have gained a lot from that so uh and i  know  so anytime you can join something when  you have a  a bright high school student teaching a  phd professor  yeah um or sharing that knowledge it's  it's it's a pretty special thing that's  pretty cool um and of course we've had  some uh some fun stuff going on  too um there's there are uh so many live  streams  these days that we're doing um so uh why  don't you want to  just give a little summary of uh what  kind of live streams we've got going on  oh so i'll talk about my favorite ones  right now so  um again back in in march uh april  uh steven was was again thinking like  okay we're in a position where we can  give  back at this point we have knowledge we  have resources  we have a this this environment that we  can  try to help people get through what's  currently going on in the world  and he started doing a q a session for  kids  and kids at heart every friday afternoon  where he joins the live stream and we  stream on twitch and youtube  simultaneously as well as this facebook  account  and just let people ask questions  anything from  how windmills work to if dogs could talk  you know how would we communicate with  them  some very very obscure things and some  some things that  i think even the professionals that that  tune in are like oh i didn't know that  yeah  yeah um and it's it's really accessible  and  and an environment that again just  encourages  this we're talking to other humans we're  not sitting  back getting delivered things like we're  watching tv but  but instead that that engagement that  that we are hoping for more and more  yeah and it's very uh it's very  impromptu which i appreciate  i like some of the uh stories that come  up and some of the sort of  you know side notes about things that  you know just little historical  footnotes that you might not have  realized  um goodness knows that steven's full of  them um  so um uh  yeah is there was there anything else i  guess that uh  you wanted to to talk about in terms of  you know remote work remote learning  anything that uh i i have forgotten  so i'll give you my favorite tagline so  anybody that's worked in any of my  groups in the last 10 years has heard me  say  we'll figure it out yes um  and and that's what we've done and i  think that's what  where we are in history with technology  and the technology stacks that we've  built and other people have built  really enables things that um that you  may not have dreamed were needed  at any point in time for instance high  fidelity  it's this spatial audio space and  one of the things that that we were  trying to think like  okay what's missing from this event now  that it has transitioned to virtual  and the number one thing is the hallway  chats the  i'm walking to get a glass of water  somebody's going somewhere else we bump  into each other and all of a sudden  there's this  this connection over an idea or um  a problem that we both want to solve and  and those don't happen virtually  because you've got 50 people in little  boxes  and everybody's on mute um and in  those those bump-ins just don't happen  so we  we use high fidelity i think first for  our company meeting  which we normally have every june and  everybody goes to the park across the  street from  from the headquarter building and and  you just get to have those  oh what are you working on these days  you know maybe our paths haven't crossed  in the last year because  you know we're working in in different  silos um  but the high fidelity space will has  allowed us to  bump into people and have little pockets  of conversations  without having to reach out like i  didn't know i wanted to talk to you but  now that i'm talking to you this is a  good thing  i wouldn't have scheduled it but i got  to bump into you  in in this space that that really  fosters  the the small individual group chat so  that is one thing that i thought i  thought was pretty neat that that we've  been trying to add into  more and more of our yeah yeah i know um  when they use the  high fidelity platform for the data  science boot camp  uh reception i ended up sort of getting  into a lot of conversations that i  wasn't really expecting to  um just because you know people might  approach you you might overhear  something  off to the side uh and that virtual uh  sound environment really gets me every  time you sort of sit and listen and  and it does kind of feel like you're  just in a room at a conference  or something you know it's just uh  having people talk around you  so um but i do i do like that uh we'll  figure it out  attitude i think that seems like a good  uh a good sort of ending note  um we whatever it is uh all the  complications that come with  being on lockdown and everyone going  remote we'll figure it out  so um thanks a lot for uh for talking  with me  anyone watching at home check out some  of the live streams  on our wolfram research youtube channel  join the next study group session  you can you can look out on wolfram.com  we'll have all the news  about all that stuff posted and keep an  eye out for  any other wolf from you or any other  events that we've got coming up  so uh thanks a lot danielle thank you  thanks for joining us you can check out  other wolfram tech talks live coding  streams and more  on the wolfram research youtube channel  or see what else is new at wolfram.com  you

